  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nStrong closed midlevel cyclone was departing through eastern\nKansas at midday, with the cyclone's circulation clearly evident\non satellite imagery. Widespread wraparound stratocumulus at \n11 am will continue to gradually erode through mid afternoon as\nsubsidence takes control, followed by rapid clearing to a clear\nsky around sunset. Strong northwest winds gusting near 40 mph \nas of 11 am, will remain strong through about 2 pm, then rapidly\nweaken to light 5-10 mph around sunset. Strong radiational\ncooling is expected through sunrise Wednesday, with a clear sky,\nlight winds and drier air, allowing temperatures to fall to\nseasonably cold mid to upper 30s. Widespread frost is \nanticipated across northwest zones Wednesday morning, where \nminimum temperatures of 32-34 will be colocated with wet \ntopsoils from last night's heavy rainfall. No hard freezes \n(<28 degrees) are expected. \n\nFantastic fall weather expected Wednesday beneath very brief\nshortwave ridging between systems. Full sunshine will prevail\nfor most of Wednesday, with only light and variable winds by\nafternoon, and afternoon temperatures near normal in the \nlower 60s. Midlevel clouds are expected to increase rapidly near\nsunset as the next shortwave approaches. \n\nCompact progressive shortwave is forecast to be centered near\nthe Colorado Rockies 6 pm Wednesday, over SW KS 6 am Thursday, \nand near Tulsa, Oklahoma 6 pm Thursday. While small, quick and\nmoisture starved, this shortwave will possess rather impressive\nkinematic forcing for ascent and frontogenesis to produce\nscattered to numerous rain showers. NBM pops are correctly\nfocused on the Wednesday night/early Thursday time frame, and\nhave increased into the likely category (60-80% coverage). \nModels have shown consistency with a track that places the best\nrain directly over SW KS, but with such a small system, any\ntrack deviation would alter rainfall amounts noticeably. \nCurrent WPC QPF guidance of 0.10-0.30 inch appears reasonable,\nwith the least amounts across the northern I-70 corridor.\nTemperatures in the upper 30s and lower 40s will keep all\nprecipitation rain, with no impacts expected. \n\nAfterwards, a long stretch of dry weather, with little to no\nimpacts, is expected heading into mid November. The next\nshortwave is progged to quickly cutoff into Mexico, and remain\nwell south of SW KS through this weekend into next week. All\nNBM pops are dry for all zones of SW KS Friday through Tuesday. \nAfternoon temperatures will be seasonably mild and pleasant in\nthe 60s, with still no cold air intrusions and no killing\nfreezes evident through at least November 10th.",
